    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we need to use the concept of independent events. The probability of two independent events occurring together is the product of their individual probabilities.\n\nLet's denote the event of it raining on Saturday as \\( A \\) and the event of it raining on Sunday as \\( B \\). We are given:\n\\[ P(A) = 60\\% = 0.60 \\]\n\\[ P(B) = 25\\% = 0.25 \\]\n\nSince the events are independent, the probability that it will rain on both days, \\( P(A \\cap B) \\), is:\n\\[ P(A \\cap B) = P(A) \\times P(B) \\]\n\nSubstituting the given probabilities into the formula, we get:\n\\[ P(A \\cap B) = 0.60 \\times 0.25 \\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication:\n\\[ 0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15 \\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we multiply by 100:\n\\[ 0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
