    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we need to use the fact that the probability of two independent events both occurring is the product of their individual probabilities. Let's denote the probability that it will rain on Saturday as \\( P(S) \\) and the probability that it will rain on Sunday as \\( P(U) \\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ P(S) = 60\\% = 0.60 \\]\n\\[ P(U) = 25\\% = 0.25 \\]\n\nSince the events are independent, the probability that it will rain on both days is:\n\\[ P(S \\cap U) = P(S) \\times P(U) \\]\n\nSubstituting the given probabilities:\n\\[ P(S \\cap U) = 0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15 \\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we multiply by 100:\n\\[ 0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
