    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The probability of rain on Saturday is $60\\%$, or $0.6$, and the probability of rain on Sunday is $25\\%$, or $0.25$. Since the probability of rain on any given day is independent of the weather on any other day, the probability of rain on both days is the product of the probabilities of rain on each day:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{Rain on Saturday and Sunday}) = P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) \\times P(\\text{Rain on Sunday})\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{Rain on Saturday and Sunday}) = 0.6 \\times 0.25\n\\]\n\nMultiplying these probabilities:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{Rain on Saturday and Sunday}) = 0.15\n\\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we multiply by $100\\%$:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{Rain on Saturday and Sunday}) = 0.15 \\times 100\\% = 15\\%\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday is $\\boxed{15\\%}$.",
